From: mklab <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH 48/48] TODO 17.5 cause 2: the bootstrap baked HOST pointers into the image

Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc

With the build date pinned (patch 47), two amd64 builds of one tree still
differed -- 79 bytes in the dictionary and 65 in openbios-builtin.elf32. The
cell holding `end-mem` read

    build A   0000 7322 4a7d e018
    build B   0000 70d9 f729 c018

which are canonical Linux userspace addresses.

WHAT IS HAPPENING. init_memory() pushes the bounds of the HOST's 1 MiB Forth
arena, and initialize-forth stores pointers into that arena in ordinary
dictionary cells: start-mem, end-mem, free-list, the pockets, prep-dict,
prep-here, /options' string values. Fourteen of them. They are NOT relocatable
-- the relocation table covers pointers into the DICTIONARY, and these point
into a different allocation entirely -- so they are written out raw and move
with the host's ASLR on every build.

WHY x86 NEVER SHOWED IT, which is the part that explains the whole asymmetry:
when the target cell is narrower than a host pointer, cross.h's pointer2cell
SUBTRACTS base_address, so what lands in the image is a small stable offset. At
equal widths the raw address is stored. arch/x86 was reproducible for a reason,
not by luck.

ZEROING THEM IS SAFE BECAUSE THEY ARE DEAD DATA. Every one is written again
before it is read: the C core enters initialize-of (or initialize, for a staged
bootstrap dictionary), which calls initialize-forth, which calls init-mem,
init-pockets and init-tmp-comp. The image's copies have never been the values
the firmware runs on.

SCOPED TO THE BUILDS THAT HAVE THE DEFECT, AND THE FIRST DRAFT WAS NOT. Without
the #ifndef, arena_lo comes out as 0 on the narrower-target builds, the window
becomes [0, 1 MiB), and every small integer matches: building x86 scrubbed 4530
cells and the bootstrap segfaulted on the next stage. The x86 build caught it
immediately, and the comment claiming the range "cannot collide with a real
value" was simply wrong -- it is now scoped, and the arena_lo > MEMORY_SIZE test
is kept as a readable second guard.

ONE IMPLEMENTATION, TWO WRITERS, and that too was found rather than foreseen.
Scrubbing only write_dictionary() left openbios-builtin.elf32 still differing by
30 bytes: write_dictionary_hex() emits the C array that gets compiled into the
builtin ELF, and it reads the live dictionary cell by cell. The rule now lives
in scrub_host_arena_ptr() and both writers call it. In the hex writer it is
applied only to the NON-relocatable branch, because a cell the relocation table
marked is a dictionary pointer rather than an arena one.

MEASURED AFTER: both arches rebuild byte-identically with SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
pinned -- dictionaries, multiboot images and the builtin ELF alike -- and
tools/openbios-check-reproducible.sh now carries a real negative control (one
build with the variable UNSET, watched to differ) in place of the known-gap row
this closes.

--- a/kernel/bootstrap.c
+++ b/kernel/bootstrap.c
@@ -140,6 +140,49 @@
 	relocation_address=reloc_table;
 }
 
+/* TODO 17.5, cause 2: the HOST's own working pointers, scrubbed out of anything
+ * we write.
+ *
+ * init_memory() hands initialize-forth the bounds of the host's Forth arena, and
+ * the bootstrap then stores pointers into that arena in ordinary dictionary
+ * cells -- start-mem, end-mem, free-list, the pockets, prep-dict/prep-here,
+ * /options' string values. Fourteen of them in the amd64 image, measured. They
+ * are NOT relocatable: the relocation table covers pointers into the
+ * DICTIONARY, and these point into a different allocation entirely, so they are
+ * written out raw and move with the host's ASLR on every build. That was the
+ * whole of the remaining difference between two builds of one tree.
+ *
+ * ZEROING THEM IS SAFE BECAUSE THEY ARE DEAD DATA. Every one is written again
+ * before it is read: the C core enters initialize-of (or initialize, for a
+ * staged bootstrap dictionary), which calls initialize-forth, which calls
+ * init-mem, init-pockets and init-tmp-comp. The image's copies have never been
+ * the values the firmware runs on.
+ *
+ * SCOPED TO THE BUILDS THAT HAVE THE DEFECT, and the first draft was not. When
+ * the target cell is narrower than a host pointer, cross.h's pointer2cell
+ * SUBTRACTS base_address -- which is the arena's own address -- so arena_lo
+ * comes out as 0, the window becomes [0, 1 MiB), and every small integer in the
+ * dictionary matches. Measured: building x86 that way scrubbed 4530 cells and
+ * the bootstrap segfaulted on the next stage. There is also nothing to fix
+ * there, because those builds already store stable offsets rather than host
+ * addresses. The arena_lo > MEMORY_SIZE test is deliberately redundant with the
+ * #ifndef: it makes the safety readable without knowing what the macro means.
+ *
+ * ONE IMPLEMENTATION, TWO WRITERS. write_dictionary() emits the .dict file and
+ * write_dictionary_hex() emits the C array that gets compiled into
+ * openbios-builtin.elf; scrubbing only the first left the ELF still differing
+ * by 30 bytes between builds, which is how this was found. */
+static ucell scrub_host_arena_ptr(ucell v)
+{
+#ifndef NATIVE_BITWIDTH_SMALLER_THAN_HOST_BITWIDTH
+	ucell arena_lo = pointer2cell(memory);
+
+	if (arena_lo > MEMORY_SIZE && v >= arena_lo && v < arena_lo + MEMORY_SIZE)
+		return 0;
+#endif
+	return v;
+}
+
 static void write_dictionary(const char *filename)
 {
 	FILE *f;
@@ -190,6 +233,26 @@
 	walk_data=write_data+sizeof(dictionary_header_t);
 	memcpy (walk_data, dict, dicthead);
 
+	/* The image is the COPY, not the live dictionary: a staged build keeps
+	 * interpreting out of `dict` after this function returns. */
+	{
+		unsigned int off, scrubbed = 0;
+
+		for (off = 0; off + sizeof(ucell) <= (unsigned int)dicthead;
+		     off += sizeof(ucell)) {
+			ucell *c = (ucell *)(walk_data + off);
+			ucell v = read_ucell(c);
+
+			if (scrub_host_arena_ptr(v) != v) {
+				write_ucell(c, 0);
+				scrubbed++;
+			}
+		}
+		if (scrubbed)
+			printk("  scrubbed %u host-arena pointer(s) from the image\n",
+			       scrubbed);
+	}
+
 	/*
 	 * prepare relocation data.
 	 * relocation_address is zero when writing a dictionary core.
@@ -275,7 +338,9 @@
             fprintf(f, "DICTIONARY_BASE + 0x%" FMT_CELL_x
                     ",\n", val);
         } else {
-            fprintf(f, "0x%" FMT_CELL_x",\n", val);
+            /* Non-relocatable: a dictionary pointer would have been marked
+             * above, so this is the branch a host-arena pointer lands in. */
+            fprintf(f, "0x%" FMT_CELL_x",\n", scrub_host_arena_ptr(val));
         }
     }
 
